d war in the 20th century  yeah and the president today did not as  you noted um  say that we would never go back that  american troops would never be called  back into that arena  the washington examiner writing about  that said this the implication  should the taliban fight their way back  to running the country  washington might conclude that it is  acceptable so long as it does not harbor  extremist groups  such as al qaeda the islamic state group  or others that might plot attacks  on the u.s its interests abroad or close  allies but bill it seems that throws us  back into the same calculations we're  always having to make  about how much we let the quote-unquote  bad guys  have a say or you know a hand in running  the country when we have worries about  things that will impact our interests or  those of our allies  yeah look shannon i was in afghanistan  with the mujadin  way back before this war in the first  war in afghanistan when they were  fighting the soviets  and you know i'm old enough to i'm old  enough to remember vietnam  i'm old enough to remember um you know  the reformation of like isis  after we led the area so i i wish i  could be optimistic  but i can't the last time we thought  afghanistan didn't matter  you know they came and knocked down two  of our buildings  in manhattan and attacked the pentagon  and i just don't believe  that the air capabilities are going to  be sufficient look what happened in  benghazi  because our flight it took hours for  flights to get there  i just don't think you can substitute  for troops on the grounds we had i  believe  three casualties this year none of them  combat related  you know we're still in germany after  defeating hitler  and we're in korea and japan after world  war  ii and that's been to our benefit to  help stabilize the region  i i just think this is going to go down  as a terrible mistake  well the president did talk too about  these calculations about why to get out  how to get out when to get